-- title:
--   Theorem 3.7, p. 21 — Algorithm 3.6 converges strongly to a primal solution, a dual solution and a Kuhn–Tucker point of Problem 1.1
-- statement:
--   Consider the setting of Problem 1.1 (finite families of real Hilbert spaces $(\mathcal H_i)_{i\in I}$, $(\mathcal G_k)_{k\in K}$ and operators satisfying [a]–[e]), and let $\mathscr P$, $\mathscr D$ and $\mathsf Z$ be the primal solution set (1.1), the dual solution set (1.2) and the Kuhn–Tucker set (2.13). Suppose that Assumptions 3.1–3.3 hold: $\alpha=\min\{\alpha^c_i,\beta^c_k,\delta^c_k\}$, $\sigma>1/(4\alpha)$ and $\varepsilon\in\left]0,1\right[$ satisfy (3.1), the step sizes $\gamma_{i,n},\mu_{k,n},\nu_{k,n},\sigma_{k,n}$ lie in the intervals of Assumption 3.1, the activated blocks $I_n\subset I$, $K_n\subset K$ are nonempty with $I_0=I$, $K_0=K$ and every block activated at least once in each window of $P+1$ consecutive iterations, and the delays satisfy $n-T\leqslant\pi_i(n)\leqslant n$, $n-T\leqslant\omega_k(n)\leqslant n$. Let $(x_n,y_n,z_n,v^*_n)$ together with $a_n,a^*_n,b_n,d_n,e^*_n,q^*_n,t^*_n,e_n,p^*_n,\Delta_n,\kappa_n,\lambda_n$ be generated by Algorithm 3.6, i.e. the block-iterative lines of Algorithm 3.4 followed, when $\Delta_n>0$, by
--   $$(\kappa_n,\lambda_n)=\Xi(\Delta_n,\tau_n,\varsigma_n,\chi_n),\qquad x_{i,n+1}=(1-\kappa_n)x_{i,0}+\kappa_nx_{i,n}-\lambda_np^*_{i,n},$$
--   and the analogous updates of $y_{k,n},z_{k,n},v^*_{k,n}$ (3.56). Suppose that the dual problem (1.2) has a solution. Then:
--
--   1. for every $i\in I$, $\sum_n\|x_{i,n+1}-x_{i,n}\|^2<+\infty$;
--   2. for every $k\in K$, $\sum_n\|y_{k,n+1}-y_{k,n}\|^2<+\infty$, $\sum_n\|z_{k,n+1}-z_{k,n}\|^2<+\infty$ and $\sum_n\|v^*_{k,n+1}-v^*_{k,n}\|^2<+\infty$;
--   3. for every $i\in I$ and $k\in K$, $x_{i,n}-a_{i,n}\to0$, $y_{k,n}-b_{k,n}\to0$, $z_{k,n}-d_{k,n}\to0$ and $v^*_{k,n}-e^*_{k,n}\to0$;
--   4. there exist a solution $\overline x\in\mathscr P$ of (1.1) and a solution $\overline v^*\in\mathscr D$ of (1.2) such that $(\overline x,\overline v^*)\in\mathsf Z$ and, for every $i\in I$ and $k\in K$,
--   $$x_{i,n}\to\overline x_i,\qquad a_{i,n}\to\overline x_i,\qquad v^*_{k,n}\to\overline v^*_k\qquad\text{strongly}.$$
--
--   This is the strongly convergent companion of Theorem 3.5: the same asynchronous block-iterative evaluations of the operators, with the relaxed half-space step replaced by a Haugazeau step anchored at the initial point, yield norm convergence of the primal and dual iterates.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The theorem uses the published setting (`Problem`, `Problem.Hyp`, `Assumption31`–`Assumption33`, `primalSet`, `dualSet`, `ktSet`) exactly as Theorem 3.5 does. Resolvents $J_{\gamma A_i}$, $J_{\mu B^m_k}$, $J_{\nu D^m_k}$ enter as given maps required to be the resolvent for every positive parameter. The carry-over of inactive blocks is stated at $n+1$. Assumption 3.3 is written $\pi_i(n)\leqslant n\leqslant\pi_i(n)+T$ to avoid natural-number subtraction. The relaxation field of the parameter record is unused and unconstrained, since Algorithm 3.6 has no relaxation sequence. Convergence in (iv) is norm convergence.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: resolvents J_{γA_i}, J_{μB^m_k}, J_{νD^m_k} → given maps JA, JB, JD with IsResolvent for every γ > 0; reason: a resolvent of a maximally monotone operator exists and is unique, so the hypothesis pins the maps to the paper's.
--   - encoding: carry-over a_{i,n} = a_{i,n−1} for i ∉ I_n (and the K-blocks) → stated at n + 1 for i ∉ I_{n+1}; reason: same runs, since I_0 = I and K_0 = K by Assumption 3.2.
--   - encoding: n − T ⩽ π_i(n) ⩽ n, n − T ⩽ ω_k(n) ⩽ n → π_i(n) ≤ n ≤ π_i(n) + T; reason: avoids natural-number subtraction; same condition.
--   - encoding: Ξ: ]0,+∞[ × ]0,+∞[ × ℝ × ℝ → ℝ² → `Xi` with the same three branches on ℝ⁴; reason: it is evaluated only where Δ_n > 0, where τ_n > 0 as in Proposition 2.7.
--   - encoding: κ_n, λ_n computed by (3.56) when Δ_n > 0 → sequences κ, lam constrained only when Δ n > 0; the parameter record's relaxation field and the state's θ field are unused; reason: same runs.
-- source:
--   Bùi & Combettes, Multivariate monotone inclusions in saddle form, arXiv:2002.06135v3, p. 21, Theorem 3.7; Algorithm 3.6, (3.56), pp. 19–20

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SaddleSplit_Async_Supplement_Setting

namespace SaddleSplit.Async.Supplement
open InnerProductSpace Filter Topology
open ThreeOpSplitting.Convergence DouglasRachfordPPA.GenDR

/-- Theorem 3.7, p. 21: under Assumptions 3.1–3.3, if the dual problem (1.2) has a solution, every
run of Algorithm 3.6 has square-summable increments, `x_n − a_n → 0`, `y_n − b_n → 0`,
`z_n − d_n → 0`, `v*_n − e*_n → 0`, and `x_n → x̄`, `a_n → x̄`, `v*_n → v̄*` (strongly) for a
Kuhn–Tucker point `(x̄, v̄*)` with `x̄ ∈ 𝒫` and `v̄* ∈ 𝒟`. -/
theorem theorem_3_7 {I K : Type*} [Fintype I] [DecidableEq I] [Fintype K] [DecidableEq K]
    {H : I → Type*} [∀ i, NormedAddCommGroup (H i)] [∀ i, InnerProductSpace ℝ (H i)]
    [∀ i, CompleteSpace (H i)]
    {G : K → Type*} [∀ k, NormedAddCommGroup (G k)] [∀ k, InnerProductSpace ℝ (G k)]
    [∀ k, CompleteSpace (G k)]
    (prob : Problem H G) (hprob : prob.Hyp)
    (α σ ε : ℝ) (prm : Alg34Params I K) (h31 : Assumption31 prob α σ ε prm)
    (Pw : ℕ) (h32 : Assumption32 Pw prm) (T : ℕ) (h33 : Assumption33 T prm)
    (JA : ∀ i, ℝ → H i → H i) (JB JD : ∀ k, ℝ → G k → G k)
    (hJA : ∀ i (γ : ℝ), 0 < γ → IsResolvent γ (prob.A i) (JA i γ))
    (hJB : ∀ k (γ : ℝ), 0 < γ → IsResolvent γ (prob.Bm k) (JB k γ))
    (hJD : ∀ k (γ : ℝ), 0 < γ → IsResolvent γ (prob.Dm k) (JD k γ))
    (st : Alg34State H G) (κ lam : ℕ → ℝ) (hrun : IsAlg36Run prob α prm JA JB JD st κ lam)
    (hD : (dualSet prob).Nonempty) :
    (∀ i, Summable fun n => ‖st.x (n + 1) i - st.x n i‖ ^ 2) ∧
    (∀ k, Summable (fun n => ‖st.y (n + 1) k - st.y n k‖ ^ 2) ∧
      Summable (fun n => ‖st.z (n + 1) k - st.z n k‖ ^ 2) ∧
      Summable (fun n => ‖st.v (n + 1) k - st.v n k‖ ^ 2)) ∧
    (∀ i, Tendsto (fun n => st.x n i - st.a n i) atTop (𝓝 0)) ∧
    (∀ k, Tendsto (fun n => st.y n k - st.b n k) atTop (𝓝 0) ∧
      Tendsto (fun n => st.z n k - st.d n k) atTop (𝓝 0) ∧
      Tendsto (fun n => st.v n k - st.estar n k) atTop (𝓝 0)) ∧
    ∃ xbar ∈ primalSet prob, ∃ vbar ∈ dualSet prob, (xbar, vbar) ∈ ktSet prob ∧
      (∀ i, Tendsto (fun n => st.x n i) atTop (𝓝 (xbar i)) ∧
        Tendsto (fun n => st.a n i) atTop (𝓝 (xbar i))) ∧
      ∀ k, Tendsto (fun n => st.v n k) atTop (𝓝 (vbar k)) := by sorry

end SaddleSplit.Async.Supplement
